pringernature.com L64: \nL65: cite13†Learn about institutional subscriptions†www.springernature.com L66: ## Other ways to access\nL67: \nL68: cite14†Licence this eBook for your library†single-ebooks.springernature.com L69: \nL70: cite13†Institutional subscriptions†www.springernature.com L71: ## About this book\nL72: \nL73: Do formulas exist for the solution to algebraical equations in one variable of any degree like the formulas for quadratic equations? The main aim of this book is to give new geometrical proof of Abel's theorem, as proposed by Professor V.I. Arnold. The theorem states that for general algebraical equations of a degree higher than 4, there are no formulas representing roots of these equations in terms of coefficients with only arithmetic operations and radicals.\nL74: A secondary, and more important aim of this book, is to acquaint the reader with two very important branches of modern mathematics: group theory and theory of functions of a complex variable.\nL75: \nL76: This book also has the added bonus of an extensive appendix devoted to the differential Galois theory, written by Professor A.G. Khovanskii.\nL77: As this text has been written assuming no specialist prior knowledge and is composed of definitions, examples, problems and solutions, it is suitable for self-study or teaching students of mathematics, from high school to graduate.\nL78: ### Similar content being viewed by others\nL79: \nL80: cite15†Image†media.springernature.com L81: \nL82: ### cite16†Analyzing the Generalization Process of Fourth Graders in a Functional Thinking Context L83: \nL84: Chapter © 2026\nL85: \nL86: cite17†Image†media.springernature.com L87: \nL88: ### cite18†A Survey on the Hypertranscendence of the Solutions of the Schröder’s, Böttcher’s and Abel’s Equations L89: \nL90: Chapter © 2021\nL91: \nL92: cite19†Image†media.springernature.com L93: ### cite20†The Levi-Civita equation in function classes L94: \nL95: Article Open access 22 October 2019\nL96: \nL97: Search within this book\nL98: \nL99: [Input] Search [Input] [Input] [Input] [Input]\nL100: ## Table of contents (4 chapters)\nL101: \nL102:   1. ### Front Matter\nL103: \nL104: Pages i-xiv\nL105: \nL106: cite21†Download chapter PDF L107: \nL108:   2. ### cite22†Introduction L109: \nL110: Pages 1-7\nL111: \nL112:   3. ### cite23†Groups L113: \nL114: Pages 9-44\nL115: \nL116:   4. ### cite24†The complex numbers L117: \nL118: Pages 45-104\nL119: \nL120:   5. ### cite25†Hints, Solutions, and Answers L121: \nL122: Pages 105-220\nL123: \nL124:   6. ### Back Matter\nL125: \nL126: Pages 221-269\nL127: \nL128: cite26†Download chapter PDF L129: \nL130: cite27†Back to top L131: ## Reviews\nL132: \nL133: From the reviews:\nL134: \"This very special and brilliant text has been written for bright non-specialists in mathematics, but it leads the reader up to topical research problems in the field, and that in a masterly manner. The book is absolutely self-contained, in its own particular fashion, and it is therefore perfectly suited for self-study, ranging from advanced high school to graduate level.\nL135: No doubt, the thorough and serious working with this outstanding text could turn very beginners into creative almost-experts in the field.\" (Werner Kleinert, Zentralblatt MATH, Vol. 1065 (16), 2005)\nL136: ## Authors and affiliations\nL137: \nL138:   * ### Moscow State University, Moscow, Russia\nL139: \nL140: V.B. Alekseev\nL141: \nL142: ## Accessibility information\nL143: \nL144: Accessibility information for this book is coming soon. We're working to make it available as quickly as possible. Thank you for your patience.\nL145: ## Bibliographic information\nL146: \nL147:   * Book title: Abel’s Theorem in Problems and Solutions\nL148: \nL149:   * Book subtitle: Based on the lectures of Professor V.I. Arnold\nL150: \nL151:   * Authors: V.B. Alekseev\nL152: \nL153:   * DOI: https://doi.org/10.1007/1-4020-2187-9\nL154: \nL155:   * Publ